    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4a65a421-eca2-5700-9d60-50af165b7fd2",
      ":question/problem": "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 2 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 30 & -12 & -7 \\\\ -8 & 17 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nBy applying elementary column operations of the form $C_i:=C_i+kC_{3},$ the matrix $A$ can be transformed into the matrix $B.$ What are the elementary column operations?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To transform $A$ into $B,$ we need to make entries to the left of $a_{13}=3$ equal to $0.$\n\nTo do this, we can apply the following elementary column operations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A &=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 2 & -7 \\\\ 4 & 9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\\\ &\\xrightarrow{C_{1}:=C_{1}-3C_{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 30 & 2 & -7 \\\\ -8 & 9 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\\\ &\\xrightarrow{C_{2}:=C_{2}+2C_{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 30 & -12 & -7 \\\\ -8 & 17 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\\\ &=B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the elementary column operations are\n\n$C_{1}:=C_{1}-3C_{3}$ and $C_{2}:=C_{2}+2C_{3}$.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
